{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"The file /Users/evintunador/.claude/jobs/9a01c126/tmp/msg.txt has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TjZ2oxv2MaWsHTavc81sUV","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"worktree-capture-everything","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/capture-everything","head":"af5de76a51a896338d095b5795f19b4a402eb0c7","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:bb4f0d43-d543-48ee-8428-5bf2dafa4ade","seq":345},"id":"ev1-4789f1c2abb4462f41ffd6bd96d84665a0d03f605ce47c66388df26f972e2404","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-06T00:56:07.003Z","producer":{"session_id":"bb4f0d43-d543-48ee-8428-5bf2dafa4ade","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.222","tool":"cledger","version":"0.18.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/capture-everything","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"worktree-capture-everything","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"The file /Users/evintunador/.claude/jobs/9a01c126/tmp/msg.txt has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TjZ2oxv2MaWsHTavc81sUV","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"4770476b-c9aa-4cbf-b0f3-758c094a8a99","promptId":"afb6a096-bf02-410d-9b2a-54cf1e4372db","sessionId":"bb4f0d43-d543-48ee-8428-5bf2dafa4ade","sessionKind":"bg","session_id":"9a01c126-8f0a-4060-95f9-fe3a00a2a8c3","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4770476b-c9aa-4cbf-b0f3-758c094a8a99","timestamp":"2026-08-06T00:56:07.003Z","toolUseResult":{"content":"claude-code: bound the tail sweep to sessions cledger already tracks\n\nReview of the sweep against real transcripts, rather than the fixtures the\ntests use, turned up a scope it was never meant to have. The intent is stated\nin the commit that added it: a session cannot capture its own last lines, so\nthe *next* session in the repo closes that gap. A gap is a handful of lines,\nminutes old, and anchoring them to current HEAD is honest.\n\nWhat the code did was sweep every transcript in the project directory with no\ncursor and no recency bound. Measured on this repo's own project directory:\n6,973 events spanning three weeks of conversations, every one of them stamped\nwith a HEAD created that minute — sessions that predated the install, adopted\nsilently, anchored to a commit they were never about. That is a backfill\nwearing a gap-closer's clothes, and it is the kind of thing the ledger exists\nto not do.\n\nThe sweep now skips a transcript with no cursor. A missing cursor does not\nmean \"this session has a gap\", it means cledger never captured this session at\nall. `readCursor` returns null rather than a zeroed cursor so the two cases\nstay distinguishable — a cursor legitimately resting at line 0 is not the same\nfact. Same corpus, same hook: 1,496 events for the session that actually\nfired, 0.3s instead of 11.2s, and the gap-closing path still closes gaps.\n\nThree things found alongside it:\n\nA torn final line was lost for good. The last line of a transcript being\nwritten is half-flushed JSON; parsing failed, the line was skipped, and the\ncursor still advanced past it. Pre-existing, but the sweep goes looking for\ntails on purpose, which is exactly where torn lines live. The cursor now stops\nshort of an unterminated final line, and records the byte offset that line\nstarts at rather than the file size — recording the size would tell the next\nsweep nothing had changed and strand the very line held back. A parse failure\nanywhere else is real corruption and is still skipped, because holding the\ncursor there would stall the session forever.\n\nCursors have two writers now — a session's own hook, and any other session\nsweeping it. Written via temp file and rename, so a concurrent reader sees one\ncursor or the other and never a half-written file that parses as \"none\".\n\n`convertLine` fell back to an empty session id where the record and\npreservation paths fall back to the transcript's filename, so one line could\nland in `claude-code:` or `claude-code:<file>` depending only on which branch\nread it. They now agree. Confirmed live: lines without their own `sessionId`\nexist in real transcripts and now join the conversation they belong to.\n\nAlso drops opencode's private copy of the capture-tally merge helper in favour\nof the shared `mergeCaptureResult`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011yyjMrYMdcPPiKnPmUm8Hs\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/.claude/jobs/9a01c126/tmp/msg.txt","originalFile":"codex: preserve inter-agent ciphertext instead of dropping it\n\nStandalone `reasoning` response_items have been preserved opaquely since\n0.10.0 — that is what lets a consumer replay a codex conversation back through\nthe provider that encrypted it, across accounts. The `encrypted_content`\nblocks embedded inside `agent_message` payloads were named as a scope cut in\nthat same release and never picked up, so inter-agent sessions stayed the one\nplace capture destroyed provider-withheld material rather than sealing it.\n\nNot a corner case: 126 of 165 `agent_message` response_items in the local\ncorpus carry encrypted blocks, and inter-agent traffic is exactly the sort of\nsession whose reasoning cannot be reconstructed from the visible turns.\n\nAn inter-agent message now becomes two events at the same seq — the visible\nturn, and a sealed `reasoning`-kind sibling whose raw.data is the line reduced\nto its encrypted blocks. The pairing, and the reason distinct kinds keep the\nids from colliding, are the same as a reasoning item with a populated summary.\nThe visible turn is unchanged: it still keeps a bare\n{type: \"encrypted_content\"} marker where a sealed block was, which is what\nmakes the split visible rather than silent.\n\nThe redaction stack already exempts `.../encrypted_content` on reasoning-kind\nevents, so pattern-matching cannot corrupt a blob it cannot read; the test\nasserts the stored blob byte-for-byte, which pins that exemption too.\n\nVerified against a real rollout: all 62 encrypted agent_message items\npreserved, blobs intact at 6-8KB each.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011yyjMrYMdcPPiKnPmUm8Hs\n","structuredPatch":[{"lines":["-codex: preserve inter-agent ciphertext instead of dropping it","+claude-code: bound the tail sweep to sessions cledger already tracks"," ","-Standalone `reasoning` response_items have been preserved opaquely since","-0.10.0 — that is what lets a consumer replay a codex conversation back through","-the provider that encrypted it, across accounts. The `encrypted_content`","-blocks embedded inside `agent_message` payloads were named as a scope cut in","-that same release and never picked up, so inter-agent sessions stayed the one","-place capture destroyed provider-withheld material rather than sealing it.","+Review of the sweep against real transcripts, rather than the fixtures the","+tests use, turned up a scope it was never meant to have. The intent is stated","+in the commit that added it: a session cannot capture its own last lines, so","+the *next* session in the repo closes that gap. A gap is a handful of lines,","+minutes old, and anchoring them to current HEAD is honest."," ","-Not a corner case: 126 of 165 `agent_message` response_items in the local","-corpus carry encrypted blocks, and inter-agent traffic is exactly the sort of","-session whose reasoning cannot be reconstructed from the visible turns.","+What the code did was sweep every transcript in the project directory with no","+cursor and no recency bound. Measured 