{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Done — merged. I didn't need your help with the smoke tests; I was able to run them end-to-end against your real local transcripts in isolated scratch repos.\n\n## What I fixed\n\n**The blocking one — sweep scope.** `readCursor` now returns `null` for \"no cursor recorded\" instead of a zeroed cursor, and the sweep skips those. A missing cursor means cledger never captured that session; it doesn't mean the session has a gap. Verified live on your project directory: the first hook fire went from **6,973 events / 11.2s** (three weeks of old conversations, all anchored to a HEAD created that minute) to **1,496 events / 0.30s** — just the session that actually fired.\n\nThen I built a proper three-session matrix to prove the bound didn't break the feature: a tracked session with a stranded tail *is* swept up, a never-captured session in the same directory is left alone, and the hooked session captures normally. All three confirmed.\n\n**Torn final line.** A half-flushed last line was parsed, failed, skipped, and the cursor advanced past it — gone for good. Since the sweep goes hunting for tails on purpose, this was the wrong bug to leave. The cursor now stops short of an *unterminated* final line and records that line's byte offset rather than the file size (recording the size would tell the next sweep nothing had changed and strand the exact line being held). A parse failure anywhere else is still skipped — holding there would stall a session's capture permanently.\n\n**Cursor writes are now atomic** (temp file + rename), since the sweep gave cursors a second writer.\n\n**`convertLine`'s session-id fallback** was an empty string where the record and preservation paths fall back to the filename, so one line could land in `claude-code:` or `claude-code:<file>` depending only on which branch read it. They agree now — and your real transcripts do contain lines without their own `sessionId`, so thi