{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Sanity check done: `npm test` → 178 pass / 1 skipped / 0 fail; two isolated live smoke runs (Claude Code hook + codex rollout) against real local transcripts, no changes made to the branch.\n\n## Verdict: close, but one fix before merge\n\nThe core of the PR is solid, and I confirmed it live rather than trusting the tests. Across 6,973 Claude Code events and 1,937 codex events captured from real transcripts, **zero `unrecognized`** — the type coverage is genuinely complete for your local corpus. Sub-conversations came out correct (4 parents, 1,282 child events, sidechain `user` turns attributed `system` rather than your git identity, `attributionAgent` → `content.agent`). `resolved` digests landed on 137 of 214 file snapshots and stayed out of the id. Codex preserved 62 sealed `agent_message` blobs, 112KB of ciphertext. The `system`-line heuristic (string `content` → turn) produced only 41 turns, all genuinely conversational (`away_summary`, `/model` output) — no harness noise leaked into the default view. Second hook run: 0 events, 0.14s. Idempotent.\n\n## The one that needs fixing before merge\n\n**The catch-up sweep anchors historical sessions to today's HEAD.** Your commit message states the intent precisely: a session can't capture its own last lines, so *the next session in a repo closes the previous one's gap*. That's a tail of a few lines, minutes old — anchoring to current HEAD is fine.\n\nWhat the code actually does is sweep *every* `.jsonl` in the project directory that has no cursor, with no recency bound. My run backfilled 13 entire sessions spanning **2026-07-18 through 08-01, all 6,973 events stamped with a single HEAD created that minute**. For a ledger whose model is \"events anchored to the HEAD at capture time,\" a three-week-old conversation pinned to today's commit is a false statement, and it's not what re-anchor is for.\n\nThe fix that matches your stated intent exactly: **only sweep transcripts that already have a cursor.** A cursor means cledger was already capturing that session, so a gap is a genuine stranded tail. No cursor means the session was never cledger's to capture, and silently adopting it is a different feature.\n\nThat also collapses the second issue: **first-run latency inside a blocking Stop hook** was 11.2s here. Survivable against the 120s timeout, but in `ds4-gateway` (18 transcripts, 25MB) an upgrading user gets a multi-second freeze at end-of-turn with no explanation.\n\n## Smaller things\n\n- **Cursor races.** A's sweep now reads and writes B's cursor while B's own hook may be doing the same. `writeFile` is unlocked and non-atomic. It degrades safely (torn JSON → cursor 0 → full re-read → dedup) but that's the expensive path. Temp-file + rename is a cheap hardening.\n- **Torn tail lines are lost permanently.** In `captureTranscriptFile`, a `JSON.parse` failure `continue`s but the cursor still advances to `lines.length`. Pre-existing — but the sweep exists *specifically* to read tails, which is exactly where half-written lines live, so this PR raises its odds. Your notes say the fix is on the gemini/qwen branch; that branch is off `main`, so it isn't here.\n- **PR #4 and PR #5 will collide hard.** #5 is branched from `main`, not stacked on this. It extracts `common.ts` (`packageVersion`/`readCursor`/`writeCursor`) and restores `KNOWN_SKIPPED_LINE_TYPES`. Its generic cursor is keyed on `CURSOR_FIELD = \"lines\"` — PR #4's sweep depends on a `size` field that abstraction doesn't carry. Whoever lands second has real reconciliation work, not textual conflicts. Worth deciding the merge order now.\n- **Duplicate helper**: `mergeCaptureResult` was added to `drift.ts` and used in claude-code, but `opencode.ts` still defines its own identical `mergeResult`.\n- **`convertLine` falls back to `\"\"` for a missing `line.sessionId`** while `recordContext`/`preserve` fall back to the filename. On this machine subagent files do carry `sessionId` (I checked), so it's not live — but that field is the assumption the entire subagent-file path rests on, and the two paths disagree if it ever goes missing.\n- **Storage, for awareness**: 34MB of events from one repo's 13 sessions. Subagent turns are 30MB of that; the four new kinds are only 3.9MB. The growth this PR causes is dominated by subagent capture, not by the bookkeeping kinds.\n\n## On live smoke-testing\n\nYes — and it has already earned its keep: commit 2 of 3 exists only because a real run found two holes replayed transcripts couldn't. Every remaining gap above is live-only too. None of anchor semantics, hook latency, or cursor races is reachable from a unit test. I'd do one more real-session run after the sweep scope is bounded.\n\nresult:\nPR #4 reviewed and smoke-tested live — sound and drift-clean, but the tail-gap sweep silently backfills whole historical sessions anchored to today's HEAD (6,973 events spanning 3 weeks, 11s in a blocking hook); bound it to transcripts that already have a cursor, and decide merge order against PR #5 which conflicts structurally.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"worktree-capture-everything","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/capture-everything","head":"af5de76a51a896338d095b5795f19b4a402eb0c7","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:bb4f0d43-d543-48ee-8428-5bf2dafa4ade","seq":197},"id":"ev1-b1e35e06ff45d6c5e068fc7658eeb28e324d2884839c4e8a5233b89125f9c7de","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-06T00:30:20.101Z","producer":{"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"bb4f0d43-d543-48ee-8428-5bf2dafa4ade","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.222","tool":"cledger","version":"0.18.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/capture-everything","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"worktree-capt